// Erasing the character under the cursor. The console keeps a column and a row
// in `con_st` and offers no way to move backwards, so the shell moves the
// column itself -- the same `wset` the console uses on its own state, because
// an exported list is the only mutable thing this language has.
//
// At column zero there is nothing to erase and nowhere to go; a wrapped line
// loses its tail rather than backing up onto the row above, which is the one
// place this is visibly not a terminal.
sh_erase() {
  word x = con_at(0);
  if(x > 0) {
    sh_wipe(x);
  }
} return void;
given boot_gfx ():() then calls sh_wipe:[0]
given boot_gfx ():() then prints:("")

// Back one, draw a space, back one again: drawing the space is what advances
// the column, so the second write is not a repetition of the first.
sh_wipe(word x) {
  wset((import con_st), 0, x - 1);
  con_ch(32);
  wset((import con_st), 0, x - 1);
} return void;
given boot_gfx (1):() then calls con_ch:(32)
given boot_gfx (5):() then calls con_ch:(32)

// The typed characters as a string, in one copy rather than n of them: the
// bytes are written into the flat store and `str_of_mem` takes the run.
//
// One byte more than the line is reserved because a zero-length line would
// otherwise ask for nothing, and `alloc` promises an address rather than a
// size (docs/SPEC.md 6).
sh_str(int[] xs) {
  int len_v = len(xs);
  word p = alloc(len_v + 1);
  string ret_s = sh_str_tail(xs, p, len_v);
} return string ret_s;
([72, 105]):("Hi")
([79, 75]):("OK")
